-- **A device handle bound by `let`, under a name another function takes as a POINTER.**
--
-- Found on 2026-09-02 while wiring `at port`, and it is **older than that work and not about
-- the port space**: the same shape at `at mmio` emits from the binary of `178e260` as
--
--     Com1 c = (Com1){ .basis = (volatile uint8_t *)(uintptr_t)1016 };
--     (*(volatile uint8_t *)(c->basis + 0)) = b;
--
-- — `c` is a VALUE and the artefact writes `c->basis`. `gabbro pruefe` says nothing,
-- `gabbro emit` returns 0, and `cc` says *`invalid type argument of '->'`*. The one reader is
-- the C compiler, and `emit.rs` already refuses to delegate that answer elsewhere: the note
-- beside the `static` of a record says so in as many words.
--
-- THE CAUSE, AND IT IS A HALF-APPLIED RULE
-- ----------------------------------------
-- `geraetezeiger` is built over the WHOLE unit and is conservative by TYPE: a name drops out
-- only if two functions declare it at two *different* device types. Here both mean `Com1`, so
-- nothing collides and `c -> Com1` stands. `ort` asks that map before `geraetewerte`, so the
-- pointer answer wins over the local one.
--
-- The parameter loop of `eigene_sicht` has the rule and writes it in one line — *„erst
-- loeschen, dann eintragen"* — and the `let` loop next to it did only the second half. **A
-- rule stated once and applied at one of its two sites** is the shape this folder pays for
-- again and again.
--
-- WHY IT NEEDS A NAME COLLISION TO SHOW
-- -------------------------------------
-- Without a second function using the same name as a pointer, `geraetezeiger` never learns
-- the name at all and `geraetewerte` answers. `beispiele/09-ohne-zeiger.gab` writes
-- `let v = Vtd(basis);` and is right for exactly that reason. *A defect that needs two
-- declarations to appear is invisible to a corpus of one-declaration examples.*
--
-- Both spaces stand here, because the artefact differs between them and the defect does not:
-- at `mmio` the handle is dereferenced with the wrong operator, at `port` the accessor is
-- handed a value where an address was owed (`Com1_THR_out(c, …)` instead of `&c`).
module probe::griffname {

assume portraum_ist_x86 arch x86_64
    "The port space is reached by `in`/`out`, and those instructions exist on x86 only."
    falsifier sonde_portraum;

static mut GERAET : u32 = 0;

device Mem(basis : u64) at mmio {
    reg DATA : u8 @0x00 class rw
}

device Tor(basis : u16) at port {
    reg DATA : u8 @0x00 class rw
}

-- The two names that also stand as `let` bindings below.
impl fn mem_lesen(m : ptr<mmio, rw> Mem) -> u8
    effects { reads m.DATA }
    costs   <= 2 ops
{
    return m.DATA;
}

impl fn tor_lesen(t : ptr<port, rw> Tor) -> u8
    effects { reads t.DATA }
    costs   <= 2 ops
{
    return t.DATA;
}

-- And the same names, bound here as VALUES.
impl fn mem_bauen(b : u8) -> u8
    effects { writes GERAET }
    costs   <= 8 ops
{
    let m = Mem(0xfee00000);
    m.DATA = b;
    return m.DATA;
}

impl fn tor_bauen(b : u8) -> u8
    effects { writes GERAET }
    costs   <= 8 ops
{
    let t = Tor(0x3f8);
    t.DATA = b;
    return t.DATA;
}

}
